      "name": "ClearGlobalStringBufferTail",
      "signature": "void ClearGlobalStringBufferTail(void)",
      "calling_convention": "__stdcall",
      "return_type": "void",
      "comment": "Clears the tail portion of the global string buffer starting at offset 0x340 for security purposes.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Calculate starting address: g_abGlobalStringBuffer + 0x340 (offset 832 decimal)\n2. Initialize DWORD counter to 0x45 (69 decimal) for 276 bytes total\n3. Zero EAX register to prepare null DWORD pattern\n4. Use REP STOSD to efficiently store 69 null DWORDs starting at calculated address\n5. Clear 276 bytes total from offset 832 to offset 1107 in the global string buffer\n\nParameters:\nNone\n\nReturns:\nvoid - No return value\n\nSpecial Cases:\n- Buffer clearing extends from offset 832 to 1107, which approaches the 1024-byte buffer limit\n- Called by cryptographic functions (DecryptDataBlocks, HashPasswordWithSalt) for secure cleanup\n- Uses optimized REP STOSD instruction for fast 4-byte aligned memory clearing\n\nMagic Numbers Reference:\n0x340 (832 decimal) - Start offset in global string buffer for tail clearing\n0x45 (69 decimal) - Number of DWORDs to clear, resulting in 276 bytes total\n0x6ff39d30 - Calculated absolute address (g_abGlobalStringBuffer + 0x340)",
