      "comment": "Structured Exception Handling (SEH) cleanup and restoration on function return.\n\nThis function properly restores the Windows SEH exception handler chain when\nan exception occurs or during function unwinding in C++ exception handling.\nIt is part of the C++ runtime exception handling infrastructure.\n\nAlgorithm:\n1. Load current exception handler list head from FS:[0] segment register\n2. Install new exception handler frame on stack with sentinel (-1), handler\n   callback address, and pointer to previous exception list\n3. Update FS:[0] to point to the new exception frame on stack\n4. Load exception record pointer from pExceptionPointers parameter (+0x8 offset)\n5. Test if exception record pointer is non-null\n6. If non-null, call DeallocateMemoryBlock with exception record, source info\n7. Restore FS:[0] to point to previous exception list from saved value\n8. Clean up 12 bytes from stack (3 pushed values) and return\n\nParameters:\npExceptionPointers: Pointer to _EXCEPTION_POINTERS structure containing\n                    ExceptionRecord at offset +0x8 and ContextRecord at +0x4\n\nReturns:\nvoid - Exception handling context is properly restored and chain is intact\n\nSpecial Cases:\n- Uses Structured Exception Handling (SEH) with FS:[0] to maintain Windows\n  exception handler chain. The sentinel value 0xffffffff marks exception frame.\n- If ExceptionRecord is null (pExceptionPointers[1]), exception processing is\n  skipped and control flows directly to cleanup.\n- Uses __stdcall convention: callee cleans 4 bytes from stack (RET 0x4)\n- Handler callback at g_ExceptionHandlerCallback is installed but may not be\n  called if exception record is null (optimization for certain error paths)\n- Stack frame temporarily modified via FS:[0] for exception chain management",
      "name_source": "LoD/1.11",
